@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Localization
@inject IHtmlLocalizer<Diten.Web.SharedResource> SharedLocalizer
@*
    BL-047b — the ONE delivery path for the DataTable chrome's language.

    The vendor supplies "Showing 1 to 9 of 9 entries", the pager and the empty-table sentence in English, and
    overrides them only when the config carries a translation. SharedResource has carried all six strings in all
    seven languages for a long time; what did not exist was a way for them to reach the 61 files that build a
    table. BL-047 fixed WorkCenterNext by seeding its own payload — so the next screen (a Turkish
    /Tasks/FieldDefinitions, measured 2026-08-10) was born English.

    This is rendered ONCE by each layout, beside dt-defaults.js, which reads it lazily. Nothing is per-page: a
    page that must remember to include something is a page that will forget, which is the whole defect.

    A page that wants its OWN wording still wins — dt-defaults reads `window.L10n` first, key by key.
*@
@{
    string[] dtKeys = ["DtInfo", "DtInfoEmpty", "DtInfoFiltered", "DtEmptyTable", "DtNoRecords", "DtZeroRecords"];
    var dtPayload = new Dictionary<string, string>();
    foreach (var key in dtKeys)
    {
        var localized = SharedLocalizer[key];
        // A missing resource returns its own KEY as the value. Writing that through would print "DtInfo" on
        // screen — worse than the English it replaced, and unreadable in every language.
        if (!localized.IsResourceNotFound)
        {
            dtPayload[key] = localized.Value;
        }
    }
}
<script id="datatable-l10n" type="application/json">@Html.Raw(System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.Serialize(dtPayload))</script>
